remote-as-filter` to permit 65010 and 65020. What is required?',['Unset `remote-as` because it is mutually exclusive with `remote-as-filter`','Keep both; the filter only augments the fixed ASN','Change local AS to 65020','Enable route-reflector-client first'],0,'Fortinet documents the fixed remote-as and remote-as-filter options as mutually exclusive.','This is a neighbor-group configuration constraint, not a route-policy decision.'),
('Fortinet','BGP Community Lists and Route-Map Matching','policy reasoning','F_COMM_REFERENCE','A community-list correctly matches 65000:100, yet no routing action occurs when that value appears. Which dependency is missing?',['A route-map rule must reference the list with `match-community`','The community-list must be assigned to an SD-WAN member directly','BGP must be converted to RIP before matching','The list must be attached to an OSPF interface'],0,'The community-list is only a match object; route-map policy must consume it.','Define-versus-use is the key policy dependency.'),
('Arista Networks','Segment Routing Traffic Engineering Policy','packet reasoning','A_SRTE_STACK','An SR Policy reports operational, but ingress capture shows a SID stack that omits the intended adjacency SID. What state is wrong?',['The active candidate path/segment list actually imposed by the headend','The MLAG reload-delay timer','The IGMP snooping membership table','The BGP route-dampening penalty'],0,'Traffic follows the segment list imposed by the headend; an operational policy can still steer incorrectly if the selected stack is wrong.','Validate the active candidate and data-plane stack, not only policy existence.'),
('Arista Networks','MLAG Reload Delay','state machine','A_MLAG_RELOAD','A rebooted MLAG peer has peer-link connectivity, but client-facing MLAG links remain down for several minutes and then automatically come up. Which feature explains the behavior?',['MLAG reload delay','Dual-primary detection delay','IGMP query interval','LACP system priority'],0,'Reload delay intentionally holds forwarding links down after reboot while state/hardware initializes.','Healthy peer-link state does not imply the recovery hold has expired.'),
('Arista Networks','IGMP Snooping Querier','configuration review','A_IGMP_QUERIER_ADDR','Global `ip igmp snooping querier` is enabled in an L2-only VLAN but the switch emits no useful queries. Which prerequisite is most likely missing?',['A configured global or VLAN querier source address','A BGP router ID','An EVPN route target','An OSPF area range'],0,'EOS requires a querier address; global enable alone does not invent the source identity.','Separate querier enablement from the source-address prerequisite.'),
('FRRouting (FRR)','PIM Source-Specific Multicast Range','protocol mechanics','R_PIM_SSM_RANGE','An FRR interface is sparse-dense, but group 232.2.2.2 matches the default SSM range. Which PIM model applies?',['PIM-SSM for that group regardless of the general sparse/dense setting','Dense mode because interface mode always wins','ASM through the RP because every sparse-capable interface uses RP','No multicast until BGP advertises the source'],0,'FRR explicitly treats groups in the SSM range as SSM regardless of the broad PIM mode.','Group-range classification can override the interface’s general sparse/dense behavior.'),
('FRRouting (FRR)','BGP Large Communities','policy reasoning','R_LCOMM_ADDITIVE','A route already has `65000:10:1`. A route-map executes `set large-community 65000:20:2` without `additive`. What should be exported?',['The new value replaces the previous Large Community set','Both values because Large Communities are always additive','No values because set actions are unsupported','The old value converted to a classic community'],0,'FRR documents non-additive `set large-community` as replacement; `additive` preserves existing values.','The important distinction is replace versus append.'),
('FRRouting (FRR)','BGP Roles and Only-To-Customer','capability negotiation','R_BGP_ROLE','Both peers configure local-role `provider`. Why can the BGP session fail before UPDATE exchange?',['The RFC 9234 Role capability reveals an incompatible relationship pairing','OTC requires the same route reflector cluster ID','Provider role changes TCP port 179 to 180','Large Community exact-match fails during OPEN'],0,'BGP Roles are negotiated during session setup and complementary relationship expectations are validated.','Role capability is session metadata, not ordinary route-map policy.'),
('Juniper Networks','BGP prefix-limit vs accepted-prefix-limit','command/output','J_PREFIX_LIMIT','A peer sends 5,000 NLRIs but import policy accepts only 100. Which Junos guard measures the raw 5,000 received prefixes?',['`prefix-limit`','`accepted-prefix-limit`','`maximum-paths`','`maximum-prefixes` only after policy'],0,'Junos prefix-limit operates on received prefixes, while accepted-prefix-limit operates after policy acceptance.','Pick the guard at the stage you intend to protect.'),
('Juniper Networks','Ethernet OAM Link Fault Management','state machine','J_LFM_ACTIVE_PASSIVE','Two Ethernet peers are configured LFM passive. What is the discovery risk?',['Neither side initiates discovery, so the OAM relationship may never start','Both sides transmit one OAM PDU every millisecond','The physical links are forced down immediately','LACP automatically becomes the OAM initiator'],0,'Passive LFM waits for the peer; at least one active initiator is needed to reliably start discovery.','Active/passive is a discovery-role state machine.'),
('Juniper Networks','LACP Fast vs Slow Periodic Negotiation','packet reasoning','J_LACP_RATE','One side requests fast LACP periodic operation and the other is configured slow. Which principle governs the transmitter cadence?',['The transmitter honors the receiver’s advertised rate/timeout request','Each side transmits only at its own local setting','The lower system priority forces fast mode','The bundle MTU determines LACP cadence'],0,'Junos documents that the transmitter honors the receiver’s rate when active/passive sides differ.','Effective periodic behavior depends on actor/partner negotiation, not only local config.'),
('Aruba/HPE','MVRP Dynamic VLAN Registration','state machine','H_MVRP_LEAVEALL','A participant sends MVRP LeaveAll. Why do valid dynamic VLANs not necessarily disappear?',['Still-needed registrations can be reasserted with Join before the Leave timer expires','LeaveAll converts them to static VLANs','STP recreates every VLAN automatically','LeaveAll is ignored for dynamic VLANs'],0,'LeaveAll creates a refresh cycle; still-valid attributes may re-register before aging.','LeaveAll is not an unconditional dynamic-VLAN purge.'),
('Aruba/HPE','VSF Split Detection','architecture','H_VSF_SPLIT','A VSF stack partitions and split detection sees both fragments. Which fragment keeps non-VSF front-plane interfaces forwarding?',['The fragment containing the primary member','The fragment with the most access ports','Both fragments until OSPF reconverges','Always the standby fragment'],0,'AOS-CX split detection keeps the primary-member fragment active and suppresses the other fragment’s front-plane ports.','The mechanism prevents dual-active forwarding.'),
('Aruba/HPE','OSPF Passive Interface Semantics','protocol mechanics','H_OSPF_PASSIVE','A passive OSPF user VLAN is configured on AOS-CX. What should remote routers normally observe?',['The subnet can still be learned through OSPF while no neighbor forms on that VLAN','The subnet is automatically suppressed from all LSAs','A Full neighbor forms without Hellos','The subnet becomes an external Type-5 route'],0,'Passive suppresses OSPF protocol packets on the interface but does not inherently suppress the connected prefix from OSPF advertisement.','Passive interface is adjacency suppression, not prefix suppression.')]
for r in current:
    v,d,typ,m,st,ch,ans,why,miss=r; add(v,d,typ,m,st,ch,ans,why,miss,U[d])
assert len(Q)==21

# 29 cumulative questions, each from a different concrete mechanism/angle and with domain-plausible distractors
hist=[
('Cisco Systems','BGP Best External','control-plane sequence','H_BEST_EXTERNAL','A border router prefers an iBGP path overall but also has two local eBGP paths. Which path can be marked Best External?',['The most-preferred candidate among the local eBGP paths','The second-best path regardless of source','Every equal-MED eBGP path','Only the eBGP path whose next hop equals the iBGP best'],0,'Best External is selected from the external-path subset, not simply the global second-best route.','Filter candidates by eBGP origin before applying the external-path ranking.','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/routers/ios/config/17-x/ip-routing/b-ip-routing/m_irg-best-external-0.html'),
('Cisco Systems','Targeted LDP','ordered troubleshooting','H_TLDP','A pseudowire depends on a non-direct LDP peer. Which dependency order is correct?',['IP reachability → targeted Hello accept